# Every reference to a layer, with what the referring key adds to it. One row per foreign key into
# `pm.layer`, and `whole` where it names the layer entire.
#
# What a lane rests on
#   This is the precondition of "a layer is a lane". `(filing, layer)` is a cell because it is the
#   coarsest key nothing refines: every table mentioning a layer either extends it with a second
#   column (`slack` with the buffer, `holder` with the kind, `draw` and `induction` with the
#   operation) or relates two whole layers (`coupling`, `part`). Not one of them names a piece of a
#   layer. If any did, a lane would have to be that finer thing, and the whole mapping would move
#   down a level.
#
# One lane set is a sound elimination
#   That is why emitting one lane set is a sound elimination rather than a simplification. The draws
#   and the inductions are two tables on the same layers, so their lane sets are two lists of the
#   same cells. The lanes either reaches are the lanes the draws reach plus the lanes the inductions
#   reach less the lanes both reach, and that overlap is worked out rather than filed: the overlap
#   of two lane sets is the set of layers, and the set of layers is a relation with a name.
#
# The buffers do not divide a lane
#   That is the third elimination, already made by the model. `pm.draw` has no `buffer` column: a
#   draw is against a layer, never against one of its three buffers, because
#   `layers/absorption.sqlc` states they are substitutes. So a draw is an operation and a layer, not
#   an operation, a layer and a buffer, and the buffer attribution nobody can make is eliminated
#   before any diagram exists. What the buffers do explain is why a lane is empty: `pm.slack` holds
#   three rows per layer, and every one of them renders as `none`.
#
# It sees the schema only
#   That is worth knowing, because the key can be lost outside it. This reads `pg_constraint`, so it
#   can show that every foreign key into `pm.layer` names both columns, and can show nothing about a
#   relation that flattens them. Two places can do that: `rank/graph_edges.sqlc` joins the two into
#   one string on purpose, because everything that reads it counts, and a count needs only distinct
#   ids; and a rule can put a claim address in the checks' own `layer` column, which
#   `checks/grain.sqlc`'s `unresolvable` count refuses. Two guards, one property: this one holds it
#   in the schema, that one in the output.
-- pg_constraint, for the foreign keys into pm.layer; the grain the lane mapping rests on.
SELECT c.conrelid::regclass::text                       AS referrer,
       pg_get_constraintdef(c.oid)                      AS reference,
       cardinality(c.confkey) = 2                       AS whole
FROM pg_constraint c
WHERE c.contype = 'f' AND c.confrelid = 'pm.layer'::regclass
